/**
@mainpage Tower Defense (COMP 345 - intermediate delivery)

@section team Team
Jana Alnoman, Chloe Fokom, Emine Nisa Mahmutogullari, Adila Burga (COMP 345).

@section overview Overview
A tower defense game in C++. Critters enter the map at one point and walk a
path to the exit. The player spends coins on towers placed on the scenery
around the path to kill the critters before they escape. This document
describes the classes and the game rules they implement. The rules follow the
COMP 345 Project Specifications.

@section rules Game rules implemented

@subsection rules_map Map
- The map is a grid of cells. A cell is scenery (Cell::canPlaceTower() is true)
  or path (Cell::isPath() is true).
- A map has exactly one entry and one exit, both on the path.
- A map is valid only if a chain of path cells (up, down, left, right) links
  the entry to the exit (Map::isValid()).
- Critters appear at the entry and walk toward the exit.

@subsection rules_towers Towers
- Towers are bought with coins and are placed on scenery cells only.
- Three types: DirectDamageTower (one target), AreaDamageTower (several
  targets at once) and SlowTower (damage plus a speed reduction).
- Every tower has a buying cost, a refund value, a range, a power and a rate
  of fire (see Tower).
- A tower can be upgraded up to level 3. An upgrade costs the buying cost
  times the current level, and adds 5 power and 1.0 range (Tower::upgrade()).

@subsection rules_critters Critters and waves
- A critter has a level, hit points, strength, speed and reward (see Critter).
- A critter dies when its hit points reach 0 and then gives its reward in
  coins to the player.
- A critter that reaches the exit steals coins from the player, equal to its
  strength.
- A slow effect multiplies a critter's speed by a factor between 0 and 1.
- Critters come in waves (CritterGroup). Each wave is harder than the previous
  one (CritterGroupGenerator).

@subsection rules_player Player
- The player starts with an amount of coins (Player).
- Coins are spent on towers and upgrades, earned by killing critters and
  lost to critters that reach the exit. The balance never goes below 0.

@section observer Observer design pattern
Map, Tower, Critter and CritterGroup inherit from Observable. Views inherit
from Observer and register with Observable::attach(). When the subject changes
it calls notifyObservers(), which calls Observer::update() on every attached
view. In this project update() only marks the view as out of date (a
"needs redraw" flag); the SFML window redraws itself on its next frame by
reading the subject's current state.

| Subject (Observable) | View (Observer)       |
|----------------------|-----------------------|
| Map                  | MapObserver           |
| Tower                | TowerObserver         |
| Critter              | CritterObserver       |
| CritterGroup         | CritterGroupObserver  |

@section playable The playable game
Game holds the rules of the playable game (placing, upgrading and selling
towers, waves, shooting, coins and lives). It has no graphics. game_main.cpp
draws it in one SFML window and turns mouse clicks and key presses into calls
on Game. The controls are listed in the README and in game_main.cpp.

@section scope Scope of this build
This is the intermediate delivery. Not implemented yet: a map editor, a
choice between several maps (the game uses one built-in map) and saving or
loading maps.

@section build Building and generating this documentation
See README.md for build and run instructions. To regenerate this
documentation, run `doxygen Doxyfile` in this folder and open
`docs/html/index.html`.
*/
